# SP+ — the advisor, and therefore Fin, may administer this machine without a
# password prompt.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Fin is the advisor's assistant and runs AS the advisor. Asked
# to add a printer, install something, or read a system log, Fin's only route is
# sudo -- and it cannot answer a password prompt. Worse, the advisor frequently
# cannot either: SP+ ships no account at all (DN-13), the account is created by
# the first-boot wizard, and a password chosen once during setup is not
# something a non-technical user reliably remembers months later. Requiring it
# turns every genuine repair into a dead end at the exact moment the advisor
# needs help.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, stated plainly. Any unconfined process running as the advisor
# is effectively root, not only Fin, and not only a person sitting at the
# machine. The wording matters: a Flatpak under bubblewrap, a unit with
# NoNewPrivileges, or anything confined by SELinux is materially different from
# an unconfined shell, so "anything with the advisor's UID" would overstate it.
# What is true is that the grant is written on %wheel while the justification
# above is written about Fin, and those are not the same set.
#
# WHAT DOES AND DOES NOT CONTAIN THIS. /usr is read-only and a bad image is one
# `bootc rollback` away. Three corrections to what this comment used to claim,
# all from an external source review on 2026-09-11:
#
#   - "Every change lands in a new deployment" was false. /etc is mutable and
#     /var is SHARED across deployments. Rollback reorders deployments; it does
#     not merge /etc edits back and it restores nothing the advisor owns. It is
#     an image undo, not a backup and not a general state restore.
#   - LUKS protects data AT REST. Locking the screen does not close an unlocked
#     mapping, so encryption is not a boundary against something already running
#     in the advisor's live session.
#   - "It removes a prompt, not a boundary" was false, and was the most
#     misleading line in this file. Authorization and intent are different
#     things. The advisor has every right to become root; malware running as the
#     advisor inherits that right without the advisor deciding anything. Saying
#     so is honesty, not mitigation.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY GATES ANYTHING, and its honest scope. The control is in Fin's
# tool path, in `fin-extensions/spplus-guardrails.ts`, not here. It gates
# irreversibility and, since 2026-09-11, persistence: a new software source, a
# signing key, a service that starts by itself, another administrator, a
# different place to get updates. The advisor is shown those and can decline.
#
# That is NOT a defence against malware holding the advisor's UID. The same
# review demonstrated five ways to synthesise a click on this desktop, one of
# which carries a literal "TODO: make secure" upstream. It IS a check on
# instructions smuggled into content Fin reads, because there the attacker is
# text, and text can ask Fin to do something but cannot answer the advisor's
# prompt. A stronger boundary needs an approval path outside the advisor's
# session, which is recorded as the target and is not built.
#
# A password prompt the user cannot answer buys none of this -- it only stops
# the assistant from helping.
%wheel ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
